e maybe there are cards that can't be evenly divided? The problem says \"each page can hold 10 cards,\" so unless there's a remainder, we just divide. Since 420 is exactly divisible by 10, 42 pages should be perfect. So the answer should be 42.\n\nHold on, but let me make sure there's no trick here. The problem says \"Punchy\" and the solution is given as a box. Let me check if my calculation is correct again. 60 packs * 7 cards = 420. Divided by 10, which is 42. Yep, that seems right. So the answer is 42. \n\nI don't think there's any other factor here. The problem seems straightforward, just basic multiplication and division. So Punger needs to buy 42 pages of cards to protect them. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe number of pages Punger needs to buy is \\boxed{42}.\n</think>\n\nPuncher collects 60 packs of baseball cards, each containing 7 cards inside. To arrange these cards in special pages, each page can hold 10 cards. \n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of baseball card cards:\n\n$$\n60 \\text{ packs} \\times 7 \\text{ cards per pack} = 420 \\text{ cards}\n$$\n\nSince there are no leftover cards, we can safely divide the total number of cards by the number of cards per page to find the number of pages:\n\n$$\n420 \\text{ cards} \\div 10 \\text{ cards per page} = 42 \\text{ pages}\n$$\n\nThus, the number of pages Punger needs to buy is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{42}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?", "answer": "60 packs of cards * 7 cards per pack = <<60*7=420>>420 cards\n420 cards / 10 cards per page = <<420/10=42>>42 pages\n#### 42", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["42"], "solution": "42"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30436, 1389, 51207, 19507, 7411, 13, 220, 1260, 49531, 220, 21, 15, 30306, 315, 19507, 7411, 13, 8886, 3769, 702, 220, 22, 7411, 4766, 13, 1260, 6801, 311, 2182, 1493, 7411, 304, 3281, 6816, 311, 6016, 279], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 30436, 1389, 6801, 311, 2182, 806, 19507, 3701, 4426, 304, 3281, 6816, 13, 6771, 752, 1490, 13, 1260, 10788, 220, 21, 15, 30306, 11, 323, 1817, 3769, 702, 220, 22, 7411, 13, 2055, 1156, 11, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 7411, 566, 702, 304, 2790, 13, 5005, 11, 21749, 429, 1372, 553, 279, 1372, 315, 7411, 1817]}
