nWait, no. Wait, the problem says: \"how many pages did he read on Wednesday?\" So the other days (Monday and Tuesday) have already been mentioned. But the total from Monday to Wednesday is 51 pages. That might be the case. Let me confirm.\n\nThe problem says: \"how many pages did he read on Wednesday?\"\n\nBut the total he read from Monday to Wednesday is 51. So, Monday:20, Tuesday:12, Wednesday: ?\n\nTherefore, add those numbers together: 20 + 12 + x = 51. Then solve for x. That would be x = 51 - 20 -12. Let me do that calculation.\n\nStart with 51 minus 20: 51 - 20 = 31.\n\nThen 31 - 12 is... 31 - 12 = 19.\n\nSo, he read 19 pages on Wednesday. Therefore, the answer is 19.\n\nWait, but let me check again. Let's make sure the problem isn't worded differently. The original problem says:\n\n\"On Monday, he reads the first book with a total of 20 pages.\"\n\n\"On Tuesday, he reads the second book with a total of 12 pages.\"\n\n\"On Wednesday, he reads the third book.\"\n\n\"If he has read a total of 51 pages from Monday to Wednesday, how many pages did he read on Wednesday?\"\n\nSo that's exactly what I used. So total pages from Monday to Wednesday is 51. Therefore, on Wednesday, he read 19 pages. Therefore, the answer is 19.\n\nBut wait, maybe I made a mistake in the calculation. Let me check again. 51 - 20 = 31, then 31 - 12 is indeed 19. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem expects that the total read from Monday to Wednesday includes the both books? Because the problem says he borrowed 3 books, and read them on each day. So each book he borrowed would have contents that he reads once, so total pages maybe?\n\nBut the problem says \"he reads the first book with a total of 20 pages.\" So perhaps each book was read with that number, so the first book's total pages is 20, the second has 12, and third maybe a different number. But the problem says that on Wednesday, he reads the third book. But since the problem says \"how many pages did he read on Wednesday?\" Then the answer is 19.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is in the translation? Let me check again. The original problem:\n\nProblem: Nico borrows 3 books from the library on Monday. On Monday, he reads the first book with a total of 20 pages. On Tuesday, he reads the second book with a total of 12 pages. On Wednesday, he reads the third book. If he has read a total of 51 pages from Monday to Wednesday, how many pages did he read on Wednesday?\n\nSolution: [This is the same as before.]\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 19.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is in the wording. For example, maybe the total he has read is 51 pages, which includes Monday to Wednesday. But if that's the case, then yes. Therefore, the calculation is correct.\n\nAlternatively, someone might think that the total is 51, so subtract 20 and 12, so 51 - 20 -12 = 19. Correct.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is 19.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{19}\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by understanding the information given:\n\n- On **Monday**, Nico reads the first book, which he has borrowed and has read with **20 pages**.\n- On **Tuesday**, he reads the **second book**, which he has borrowed and has already read with **12 pages**.\n- On **Wednesday**, he reads the **third book**, and the total number of pages he has read **from Monday to Wednesday** is **51**.\n\nSince this total (51 pages) includes the pages of both books he borrowed on Tuesday and Wednesday, we are looking for the number of pages he read on the third book.\n\nLet’s calculate the unknown:\n\n$$\n\\text{Pages on Wednesday} = \\text{Total read from Monday to Wednesday} - \\text{Pages on Monday} - \\text{Pages on Tuesday}\n$$\n$$\n= 51 - 20 - 12 = \\boxed{19}\n$$\n\n### Final Answer\n$$\n\\boxed{19}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Nico borrows 3 books from the library on Monday.  On Monday, he reads the first book with a total of 20 pages.  On Tuesday, he reads the second book with a total of 12 pages.  On Wednesday, he reads the third book.  If he has read a total of 51 pages from Monday to Wednesday, how many pages did he read on Wednesday?", "answer": "From Monday to Tuesday, Nico reads a total of 20 + 12 = <<20+12=32>>32 pages.\nOn Wednesday, he read a total of 51 - 32 = <<51-32=19>>19 pages.\n#### 19", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Nico borrows 3 books from the library on Monday.  On Monday, he reads the first book with a total of 20 pages.  On Tuesday, he reads the second book with a total of 12 pages.  On Wednesday, he reads the third book.  If he has read a total of 51 pages from Monday to Wednesday, how many pages did he read on Wednesday?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["19"], "solution": "19"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 74035, 31632, 1811, 220, 18, 6467, 504, 279, 6733, 389, 7014, 13, 220, 1913, 7014, 11, 566, 15804, 279, 1156, 2311, 448, 264, 2790, 315, 220, 17, 15, 6816, 13, 220, 1913, 7589, 11, 566, 15804, 279], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 74035, 45564, 220, 18, 6467, 11, 1290, 30, 1597, 566, 1349, 1817, 825, 389, 7014, 11, 7589, 11, 323, 7920, 13, 576, 1156, 2311, 702, 220, 17, 15, 6816, 389, 7014, 11, 279, 2086, 702, 220, 16, 17, 6816, 389, 7589, 11, 323, 279, 4843, 825, 1112, 87501, 11, 3783, 11, 279, 3491, 2727, 389, 7920]}
