y are made up of 5 pages each. How many brochures is the printing press creating?\"\n\nWait, so brochures can be arranged in any order, but they must be made up of 5 pages each. So, each brochure is a series of pages that add up to 5 pages? That seems odd, unless the brochure is divided into multiple 5-page sections. But the problem says \"made up of 5 pages each\". Maybe there's some confusion here. Alternatively, perhaps the brochures are printed with 5-page sections? Like a single sheet? But the problem mentions spreads. Maybe I need to clarify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"pages\" here refers to everything printed. So, the brochures are made up of pages (each page is printed) such that the total pages for each brochure is 5. So, if each brochure has 5 pages, then how many brochures is that? But that seems too vague. The problem mentions both the spreads and the ads. Maybe the total number of pages is the sum of the spread pages and the ad pages, and they need to be made up of 5 pages each. Wait, but how? If each brochure has 5 pages, then if you have multiple brochures, each with 5 pages, the total number of brochures would be related to the total pages. But the problem says \"the brochures can be arranged in any order... as long as they are made up of 5 pages each.\" Maybe there is a translation issue?\n\nWait, maybe there's a misunderstanding. Let me try to rephrase. The entire process of printing brochures includes printing both the spreads (singles and doubles) and the ads. Then, the brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each. But the 5 pages each refer to the pages that are printed. So, if you have a brochure made up of 5 pages, then how is that arranged? Maybe each sheet of fabric has 5 pages? But this is getting too confusing.\n\nWait, let me check the problem once again:\n\n\"A printing press is printing brochures. The press prints 20 single-page spreads, and twice as many double-page spreads. For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page. The brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each. How many brochures is the printing press creating?\"\n\nSo, the brochures' pages (the entire brochures printed per sheet?) are made up of 5 pages each. So, each brochure has exactly 5 pages. Therefore, if the printing press creates X brochures, each with 5 pages, then total pages is 5X. But then how does this relate to the spreads and the ads?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the brochures total pages is the sum of the pages printed (spreads and ads) which must be exactly 5 pages each. But each brochure is 5 pages? Therefore, the total number of brochures would be (total pages) / 5. But how does the total pages come into play with the 20 singles and 40 doubles and the ads?\n\nWait, maybe the total number of pages printed by the press is (singles and doubles) + (ads). But each brochure is made up of 5 pages. Therefore, if there are n brochures, total pages is 5n pages. But then the total pages from the presses is the sum of the 20 singles, 40 doubles, and the ads. Let me figure out the total number of pages from the presses.\n\nFirst, the singles and doubles: 20 singles + 40 doubles. So, that's 60 pages.\n\nThen, for each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4", "source_record": {"question": "A printing press is printing brochures. The press prints 20 single-page spreads, and twice as many double-page spreads. For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page. The brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each. How many brochures is the printing press creating?", "answer": "There are a total of 20 single-page spreads * 2 = <<20*2=40>>40 double-page spreads.\nAs these are made up of 2 pages each, there are 40 double-page spreads * 2 pages = <<40*2=80>>80 pages in the double-page spreads.\nIn total, there are 20 single-pages + 80 double-pages = <<20+80=100>>100 pages in the spreads.\nA block of ads is printed every 4 pages, so there will be 100 pages / 4 pages/block = <<100/4=25>>25 blocks of ads.\nThis is a total of 25 blocks * 4 ads = <<25*4=100>>100 ads.\nAs each ad takes up a quarter of a page, this must create an additional 100 ads * 0.25 = <<100*0.25=25>>25 pages.\nTherefore, the press has printed 100 pages from the spreads + 25 pages of ads = <<100+25=125>>125 pages.\nSince brochures are made up of 5 pages each, this creates a total of 125 pages / 5 = <<125/5=25>>25 brochures.\n#### 25", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: A printing press is printing brochures. The press prints 20 single-page spreads, and twice as many double-page spreads. For each 4 pages printed for the spreads, the press prints a block of 4 ads, each of which take up a quarter of a page. The brochures can be arranged in any order as long as they are made up of 5 pages each. How many brochures is the printing press creating?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["25"], "solution": "25"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 362, 18484, 3493, 374, 18484, 2896, 331, 1413, 13, 576, 3493, 23473, 220, 17, 15, 3175, 15112, 42553, 11, 323, 10917, 438, 1657, 1990, 15112, 42553, 13, 1752, 1817, 220, 19, 6816, 16709, 369, 279, 42553, 11], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 752, 1430, 311, 7071, 700, 419, 3491, 13, 88190, 11, 279, 3405, 374, 911, 264, 18484, 3493, 429, 23473, 2896, 331, 1413, 13, 576, 3493, 18644, 1045, 1372, 315, 3175, 15112, 42553, 323, 1990, 15112, 42553, 13, 5005, 11, 1052, 525, 13740, 429, 1896, 705, 264, 8338, 315, 264, 2150, 1817, 13, 576, 2896, 331, 1413, 646, 387, 27802]}
