heck the problem again.\n\nThe problem says, \"Texas has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.\" Then they added five more classes. So, maybe they added five more classes, which would each have the same number of students as before, which is 20. Therefore, the total number of students would then be original 15 classes times 20 plus 5 new classes times 20. \n\nSo, original total is 15*20 = 300. Then adding five more classes, each with 20, so 5*20 = 100. So total students would be 300 + 100 = 400. Therefore, the answer is 400. So, \\boxed{400}. Let me confirm once again. \n\nBut wait, sometimes when you add new classes, you have to consider if the students are being added to the existing count or not. But the problem doesn't specify, but it's possible that each new class has 20 students. The original problem was about 15 classes, so probably adding five more classes with 20 students each. Therefore, the total number of students is 15*20 + 5*20 = 20*(15 + 5) = 20*20 = 400. Yes, that makes sense. \n\nTherefore, the answer is \\boxed{400}.\n</think>\n\n\\boxed{400}<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?", "answer": "The school has 15 x 20 = <<15*20=300>>300 students right now.\nThey have an additional 5 x 20 = <<5*20=100>>100 students after adding 5 more classes.\nTherefore, Texas Integrated School will have a total of 300+100= <<300+100=400>>400 students.\n#### 400", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Texas Integrated School has 15 classes and has 20 students per class.  They added five more classes, how many students will they have now?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["400"], "solution": "400"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 